## Introduction
In eutherian mammals, parents have the capacity to control gene dosage in their offspring in a process known as genomic imprinting. The monoallelic expression of imprinted genes relies on cis-acting regulatory regions, known as 'imprinting control regions' (ICRs). These acquire differential DNA methylation in the oocyte and the sperm, are inherited as asymmetrically methylated by the embryo and maintain this property throughout life [1]. Life-long ICRs are sparsely distributed in mammalian genomes (only 23 have been described in mice), and control roughly 150 genes [2,3]. However, the importance of genomic imprinting is underscored by embryonic lethality or severe developmental defects in mice harboring global and gene-specific imprint defects, not to mention a number of human diseases that are associated with imprinted gene misregulation [1].
A distinguishing feature of ICRs is their ability to withstand the post-fertilization changes sweeping the DNA methylation landscape inherited from the parental gametes [4]. In the early embryo, maternally and paternally inherited DNA methylation patterns are erased genome-wide. Sperm and oocytes exhibit \(80\%\) and \(54\%\) CpG methylation, respectively; meanwhile the so-called 'ground state' cells that form the pluripotent inner cell mass (ICM) of the blastocyst reach approximately \(20\%\) CpG methylation [5]. At the time of embryonic implantation, DNA methylation patterns are reestablished prior to lineage commitment [6]. Consequently, DNA methylation asymmetries that may have existed between parental chromosomes are largely mitigated. Genomic imprints are a notable exception, as methylated alleles of ICRs escape the embryonic wave of demethylation, and their unmethylated counterparts are resistant to the subsequent wave of remethylation. However, they are not immune to DNA methylation reprogramming in all developmental and cellular contexts: parent-specific methylation imprints are naturally lost during early germline development. This is an obligate pre-requisite for the establishment of imprints in a sex-specific manner in mature gametes [7]. Imprinting can also be sensitive to culture conditions, as evidenced by the increased frequency of abnormal ICR methylation in protocols for assisted reproductive techniques [8].
## Relationships between pluripotency, DNA methylation and genomic imprints
The genome of somatic cells is heavily methylated, with 60–80% of all the CpG targets being methylated. While
maintenance of high levels of genomic methylation is essential for somatic cell survival [18], PSCs in vivo, such as ICM cells and PGCs, are notable for their low methylation, displaying \(20\%\) and \(6{-}8\%\) respectively, according to recent genome-wide bisulfite sequencing data (WGBS) [5,19,20]. This has led to the idea that relaxation of DNA methylation-based control of the genome may be required for self-renewal and pluripotency. In line with this model, the efficiency of iPSC induction is improved by treatment with cytosine methylation inhibitors [21].
The traditional culture medium of murine ESCs was originally based on fetal calf serum (FCS) and leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), which maintain self-renewal and pluripotency properties through activation of the Lif/Stat3 and Pi3K/Akt signaling pathways [22]. Interestingly, global DNA methylation of serum-grown ESCs is remarkably high — \(70\%\) of CpGs — mimicking the DNA methylation landscape of somatic cells rather than their in vivo source, the ICM cells \([14^{\bullet},15^{\bullet\bullet},16^{\bullet\bullet},19,23]\). Elevated expression of the de novo DNA methyltransferases (Dnmts) in this system is probably responsible for this DNA methylation gain \([24^{\bullet\bullet}]\). Moreover, prolonged periods of serum culture have been known for some time to promote epigenetic drift and altered allelic methylation patterns at ICRs in ESCs. Importantly, these abnormalities were shown to persist in ESC-derived mice and to lead to aberrant phenotypes [25,26].
More recently, serum-free culture techniques have been developed: in addition to LIF, they utilize inhibitory molecules against MEK and \(\mathrm{Gsk3\beta}\) kinases, which normally promote exit from pluripotency [27]. Because this two inhibitor (2i) medium is more potent than serum-based conditions in stabilizing pluripotency-associated transcriptional profile \([28^{\bullet}]\), it was suggested that the 2i ESC epigenome may more closely resemble the one of pluripotent ICM. Indeed, various quantification methods (mass spectrometry \([14^{\bullet}]\) and WGBS \([15^{\bullet\bullet},16^{\bullet\bullet}]\)) agree on the low genomic methylation level of 2i-grown ESCs: \(20-\) \(30\%\) of CpG methylation, which is in the same range as the ICM in vivo.
The direct link between 2i culture and DNA hypomethylation is repression of the de novo DNA methylation machinery, which probably occurs at the transcriptional level \([14^{\bullet},15^{\bullet\bullet}]\). The presence of 2i additives stimulates the expression of the PR-domain-containing transcriptional regulator Prmd14, which binds to the promoters of Dnmt3a, Dnmt3b and Dnmt3L and primes Polycomb-dependent deposition of repressive H3K27me3 marks \([14^{\bullet},15^{\bullet\bullet},24^{\bullet\bullet}]\). There is also evidence that 2i-based medium may enhance transcription of the Ten-eleven translocation (Tet) family of enzymes \([14^{\bullet},15^{\bullet\bullet},16^{\bullet\bullet},17^{\bullet}]\); these oxidize methylcytosines to hydroxymethylcytosines, which can serve as intermediates towards unmethylated cytosines through both
passive and active mechanisms [29–32]. Recently, supplementing media with vitamin C has been shown to further activate Tet proteins at the post-translational level \([33^{\bullet\bullet}]\). As a consequence, genomic methylation levels of 2i-grown ESCs are reduced by half after \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) of vitamin C treatment.
The tremendous and reversible \([16^{\bullet\bullet}]\) impact that culture media have on ESC methylome implies an epigenetic plasticity that is not found in somatic, differentiated cells. It also creates pause when considering the impact on genomic imprints. The DNA methylation asymmetry that exists between parental alleles is, by definition, epigenetic as opposed to genetic. Therefore, artificially modulating the expression of epigenetic regulators with culture additives might result in DNA methylation loss or gain at imprinted loci. To wit, continuous de novo Dnmt expression in serum conditions may induce DNA hypermethylation at ICRs. Conversely, by shutting down de novo DNA methylation and enhancing demethylation, 2i-based medium could jeopardize ICR methylation maintenance (Figure 1).
## Insights into the effects of ESC culture medium on genomic imprints
Several studies have examined the dynamics of DNA methylation changes occurring upon switching ESCs from serum to 2i-based conditions. Using high resolution WGBS, Ficz and colleagues demonstrated genome-wide the initial observation that ICRs can be abnormally methylated in serum-grown ESCs, with a possible bias towards methylation gain (Figure 2a) \([15^{\bullet\bullet}]\). Out of the 15 analyzed ICRs, three were particularly hypermethylated in serum (Zac1, Peg1 and H19 ICRs), while only the Gnas1A ICR displayed hypomethylation. Switching to 2i-based conditions did not change the ICR methylation profile established in serum-based conditions even after 24 days, although there was a slight trend towards lower methylation observed over all ICRs \([15^{\bullet\bullet}]\) (Figure 2a). An independent study by Habibi and colleagues confirmed overall conservation of ICR methylation status upon serum to 2i conversion, despite genome-wide DNA methylation loss \([16^{\bullet\bullet}]\). Interestingly, ICRs appear also relatively impervious to vitamin C supplementation, while the genome undergoes even more pronounced demethylation \([33^{\bullet\bullet}]\). However, the analysis in question was performed \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) of vitamin C treatment; prolonged exposure could lead to full ICR demethylation, similarly to PGCs where methylation erasure is dependent upon Tet activity [34].
Comparison of PSCs initially derived in 2i-based versus serum-based conditions has also been carried out. Leitch and colleagues reported faithful parental methylation maintenance of three analyzed ICRs in early stage EGCs derived from PGCs in 2i medium, bolstering the notion that this culture system reproduces a closer approximation
Finally, serum-grown ESCs are notorious for their sexual dimorphism regarding ICR methylation maintenance [36,37]. This peculiarity is conserved in 2i-based medium: for instance, while Peg3 and Peg10 ICRs consistently maintain proper imprints in 2i-grown male ESCs, their female counterparts display near complete DNA methylation loss, upon initial derivation in this medium or after conversion from serum-based culture. Double X chromosome dosage has been reported to inhibit MAPK and
eliminate potential cis genetic effects on DNA methylation states.
## The epigenetic heterogeneity of iPSCs
While both EGCs and ESCs are derived from in vivo niches, the third class of PSCs can be induced in vitro by ectopic expression of pluripotency factors in somatic cells [11,39]. The ability to artificially generate iPSCs provided a major breakthrough in stem cell biology, and their potential for therapeutic benefits is tantalizing. However, unlike EGCs and ESCs, iPSCs are pure creations of transgenes and culture. As there are different methodologies for iPSC generation [12,40,41], the impact on DNA methylation also exhibits variance. In particular, various studies reported that genomic imprinting is particularly susceptible in mouse and human iPSCs \([42,43,44^{\bullet\bullet},45^{\bullet\bullet}]\) . From a targeted study of six ICRs, Sun and colleagues found a fair amount of stochasticity of imprint maintenance among various iPSC clones, with equal representation of methylation gains and losses \([44^{\bullet\bullet}]\) (Figure 2b). Similarly, Takikawa and colleagues reported heterogeneous maintenance of genomic imprints with the notable exception of the Peg/ ICR, by systematically analyzing nine different ICRs in 12 iPSC clones derived from hybrid mice, to allow for parental allele distinction \([45^{\bullet\bullet}]\) . The drastic differences between individual clones from the same genetic background suggest iPSCs are particularly sensitive at the level of DNA methylation. Until iPSC protocols are developed to resolve this issue, it is clear from the studies that it is of tantamount
importance to assess the epigenetic state of a given iPSC clone before continuing experimentation.
The impact that imprint misregulation can have on iPSC physiology and potential is exemplified by the \(D l k I/G t l2\) region, a paternally imprinted gene cluster, whose function is required for proper development [46,47]. Compared to ESCs of the same genetic background, iPSCs frequently show abnormal repression and hypermethylation of the ICR of this cluster [48,49], in correlation with a failure to form high-grade chimeras [50–52]. A number of techniques were developed to circumvent this aberrant gain of DNA methylation, including vitamin C addition \([53^{\bullet}]\) . However, introducing such agents may have unforeseen effects at other loci. The most prudent method may be determining proper stoichiometry of pluripotency factor expression [54].
## Mechanisms of imprint maintenance in 2i conditions
To understand how imprints might be eroded in hypomethylating 2i culture conditions, one must examine the mechanism by which imprints are stably maintained through the wave of DNA demethylation of the early embryo. Mammals have evolved an elegant system to protect the methylated allele of ICRs, which relies on Zfp57, a Krüppel-box associated box-containing (KRAB) zinc finger protein that bridges genetic with epigenetic information. Zfp57 recognizes a six-base motif containing a CpG dinucleotide (TGCCGC); Zfp57 will only bind
this motif if the CpG is methylated [55]. Zfp57 interacts with the Kap1 protein (also known as Trim28) [56], which in turn recruits epigenetic modifiers such as Dnmts \([55,57^{\bullet}]\) and the H3K9 methyltransferase Setdb1 [58], and readers such as PGC7/Stella [59]. This concordance of heterochromatic factors is essential for maintaining methylated states at ICRs, and even protecting them against Tet-induced hydroxymethylation [34], as revealed by ICR hypomethylation in early mouse embryos and ESCs deficient for the Zfp57/Kap1 complex [60]. In humans, genetic mutations of ZFP57 cause hypomethylation of multiple ICRs and are associated with transient neonatal diabetes [61].
Misregulation of Zfp57 expression itself could perturb ICR methylation in cultured PSCs. Zfp57 expression is linked to pluripotency; it is highly expressed in oocytes and early embryos, progressively turned off upon differentiation and undetectable in somatic tissues [56,62]. The basis for the embryonic expression pattern can be explained by recent work demonstrating that Zfp57 is positively regulated the pluripotency factor Nanog [63]. Hence, perhaps ESCs are more resistant to imprint defects than iPSCs, which are derived from somatic cells less equipped for imprint protection. This could elucidate why iPSCs exhibit higher imprint instability. Alternatively, one can envision a culture condition in which misregulation of DNA methylation-related enzymes — such as Dnmt and Tet proteins — could compromise methylation of Zfp57 binding sites. Moreover, in culture systems where PSCs are dividing endlessly and where antagonistic methylation processes are continuously balanced \([64^{\bullet}]\), the chance for stochastic unfaithful DNA methylation maintenance across DNA replication may be increased. In these cases, ICR methylation maintenance factors could be displaced by proteins promoting active chromatin. For example, unmethylated CpG-rich regions can recruit zinc-finger proteins of their own, such as Cfp1 and the H3K36 demethylase Kdm2b; these facilitate the formation of chromatin complexes that could further antagonize de novo DNA methylation and would prevent restoration of DNA methylation [65,66].
Interestingly, discrete imprinted loci vary in the ability to maintain DNA methylation in all the culture systems described. Is this simply a function of the number of Zfp57 binding sites? The number of Zfp57 binding motifs ranges from 2 to 13 among the 23 known ICRs. Compiled data from multiple studies suggest that the fewer motifs may roughly correlate with vulnerability to DNA methylation loss, at least in the case of iPSCs (Figure 2b). However, the Kcnq1ot1 ICR exhibits relatively stable imprint maintenance with only four Zfp57 binding sites. The stability of certain imprints may be due to other features of the locus, such as protection by chromatin-bound proteins, or chromosome organization. Alternatively, the higher vulnerability of certain ICRs to DNA methylation changes in PSC cultures, such as the \(Dlk1/Gtl2\) locus, may be the result of cell selection. If changing the dose of the corresponding genes is beneficial to PSC physiology, cell clones with relevant methylation states will be more favorably represented among PSC cultures.
