## Conclusions
The focus of this review has been on murine PSCs, which is the primary model system for mammalian stem cell biology. Human ESCs (hESCs), though derived from the ICM, resemble lineage-primed cells, as opposed to a naïve cell type, and there are reports of ICR methylation instability \([67^{\bullet\bullet}]\). It should be noted that a new hESC culture protocol has been developed that drives a closer approximation of the human ground state, including reduced global DNA methylation [68]. However, the status of imprints in this culture system is unknown. Human somatic cells are also capable of being reprogrammed to iPSCs. As in the mouse, both global and locus-specific analyses of ICRs indicate a higher occurrence of DNA methylation abnormalities in human iPSCs versus hESCs [42,69]. Somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT) to oocytes has created yet another avenue to generate human PSCs, termed NT-ESCs [70] — though it appears that imprint abnormalities in this system are comparable with those observed in iPSCs \([71^{\bullet\bullet}]\). Given the excitement over the possibilities of stem cell therapies, it is worth continuing to examine the stability of genomic imprints in culture. There are a number of diseases that are associated with imprint misregulation, such as Prader–Willi syndrome, Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome, and neonatal diabetes, just to name some examples (reviewed in [1]). Therefore, imprinting deficiencies that exist in stem cell in culture may also hinder the effectiveness of a given therapy. PSC culture conditions should be optimized to balance the stoichiometry of methylation and demethylation activities and reduce stochastic and sometimes deleterious effects on imprinted locus methylation [54].
